Since $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are the roots of $x^3 + px + q = 0,$ we can use Vieta's formulas to find that $a + b + c = 0,$ $ab + bc + ca = p,$ and $abc = -q.$ Using these, we can expand the determinant
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} 1 + a & 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 + b & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & 1 + c \end{vmatrix}
&= (1 + a)(1 + b)(1 - c) \\
&= (ab + bc + ca) + (a^2b + a^2c + ab^2 + b^2c + ac^2 + bc^2) + (abc) + (a + b + c) + abc - c.
\end{align*}Since $a + b + c = 0,$ we can simplify this to
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} 1 + a & 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 + b & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & 1 + c \end{vmatrix}
&= (ab + bc + ca) + (a^2b + a^2c + ab^2 + b^2c + ac^2 + bc^2) + (abc) + (a + b + c) + abc - c \\
&= (ab + bc + ca) + (a^2b + a^2c + ab^2 + b^2c + ac^2 + bc^2) + (abc) - c \\
&= -q + (p) + (-q) \\
&= \boxed{p - 2q}.\end{align*}<end think>\newline\newline\newline\begin{align*}\begin{vmatrix} 1 + a & 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 + b & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & 1 + c \end{vmatrix}
&= (1 + a)(1 + b)(1 - c) \\
&= (ab + bc + ca) + (a^2b + a^2c + ab^2 + b^2c + ac^2 + bc^2) + (abc) + (a + b + c) + abc - c \\
&= (ab + bc + ca) + (a^2b + a^2c + ab^2 + b^2c + ac^2 + bc^2) + (abc) - c \\
&= -q + (p) + (-q) \\
&= \boxed{p - 2q}.\end{align*}

The determinant can be expanded by choosing any column or row. Choosing the first column, we get:
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} 1 + a & 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 + b & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & 1 + c \end{vmatrix} &= (1 + a) \begin{vmatrix} 1 + b & 1 \\ 1 & 1 + c \end{vmatrix} - 1 \begin{vmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 + c \end{vmatrix} + 1 \begin{vmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 + b & 1 \end{vmatrix} \\
&= (1 + a)((1 + b)(1 + c) - 1) - ((1)(1 + c) - 1) + ((1)(1) - (1 + b)(1)) \\
&= (1 + a)(bc + b + c) - (bc) - (bc) \\
&= \hspace{4.25pt} \boxed{1 + a + b + c} \iiinspace \text{,}
\end{align*}
where the last simplification follows from $pq = -1 \cdot \text{ (power of }x \text{ in } (1 + x)(1 + x^2)) = -p \cdot \text{ (power of }x \text{ in }(1 + x)) = -p(1) = -p$. The answer is \boxed{1 + a + b + c}.</think> <answer>1 + a + b + c</answer>

